00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:58.560
So back to the question that I started
with.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:04.040
I'm meeting dozens of engineering teams
on a weekly basis

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:08.320
from all over the world
than from different sizes, from startups

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:11.400
that are 5 to 10 people
to enterprise teams

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.520
that have 1000 2000 developers.

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:17.520
And I hear the same explanation again
and again.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.520
That code review became a bottleneck.

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:25.320
And as a good product manager, I'm trying
to understand why this is happening.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:28.320
And those kind of discovery calls, why

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:33.120
we're using the best coding agents
in the world, the most

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:37.720
costly and expensive models available.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:40.800
We are still hitting the point
where human beings need

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:43.800
to review code, where

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:48.960
engineering managers explain
that they have five times or three times

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.040
more PRs that are waiting to be merged,
but nothing is happening

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:56.360
and velocity is actually
becoming slower than faster.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.680
One of the main themes that repeated in

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:02.680
those conversations
was the matter of trust.

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:06.960
People explained
that they don't really rely

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.960
on the agent to close the feedback loop.

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:15.000
A lot of the users told us
that the problem that they had

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.840
is they have specs, and they see that
the coding agent multiple times

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:24.120
just ignores most of the specs part of it,
or when they see in the end the feature

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:28.280
that was implemented, it's not really
how they intended it to look like.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:32.120
So as a product manager, when you hear
that kind of pain, you decide,

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:33.960
okay, this is going to be my mission.

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:37.680
I hear this pain that nobody else
is solving and I'm going to solve it.

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:41.880
And what you're going to see in this
session is the journey that we did

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:45.520
to build the tools and the context
to give coding agents the ability

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:50.120
to really verify that your feature
was implemented as you asked.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:54.240
If you don't believe this issue, I'm
going to show it from an example

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:55.880
from my day to day life.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:04:00.800
So I'm a product manager,
I define I design skills, I

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:05.280
design features, I decide what the company
is going to implement.

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:07.320
And this is an example for a ticket

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:11.040
that we were supposed to implement
as part of the sprint.

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:16.240
We have as part of our product,
the ability to give our customers

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:19.920
the option
to integrate their ticketing system.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:24.040
It can be JIRA linear
and fiber is one of them.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:27.840
Not a lot of people are using it and
not a lot of people are familiar with it.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:31.360
But that was one of the features
that we had to complete.

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:33.320
And part of what we wanted to do

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:36.560
is to add this
to the onboarding flow of the product.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:42.120
So when a user is onboarding to bus,
it will have the option to integrate

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:45.200
which of the tickets ticketing systems
available that they have.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:49.800
And because I'm reviewing every product,
every feature that is being released,

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:54.320
I asked to make sure that the same bug

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:59.160
that happened when we integrated another
ticketing system won't repeat again.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:03.600
I literally added a recording
from a previous time

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:07.400
when we added another integration
where the continue button

00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:10.520
was overlapping
the new integration that was added

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:14.360
a couple of days later,
the developer sent me a slack

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:18.640
message telling me, hey,
I just completed that feature.

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:20.160
Here's the link to Preview Environment.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:23.040
Please review it. Guess what?

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:25.400
The continue button is

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.400
exactly where I asked him not to put it.

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:29.880
Guys.

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:33.040
Like,
I couldn't be more explicit than that.

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:38.320
And I'm showing this because this is a
really simple example, right?

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:44.280
Like for who of you are taking part
in being in an engineering team?

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:45.600
Product design.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:47.680
This is our day to day frustration.

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:49.280
We're writing specs.

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:52.480
They are very detailed
and we're very happy

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:55.680
to deliver them to the engineering teams
that we're working with.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:59.680
But unfortunately when we see
the results is not as we expected.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:03.360
And you can understand that
if this is such a front end

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:06.520
simple task that gone wrong,

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:10.160
how like what would be the results
if we're doing a complex task?

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:15.880
So the bottom line is that coding agents
that we are using

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:21.120
are not focused on verifying features.

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:23.920
There are amazing and generating code.

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:26.080
And I think one of the themes
that is repeated

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:30.320
in a lot of the talks here
and in other conferences is the fact that

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:35.640
it's so easy to build right now, but
the way that the coding agents are built,

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:37.440
they are focused on generating features.

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.720
They are not focused in fully extracting
all the specs that we have, all the specs

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:47.760
that we write, and they are not focused
in verifying the feature as it is.

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:51.960
And when I say verifying the feature,
I'm not meaning verifying the code.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.240
For those of you who are working in a team
and you have like

00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:00.480
your preview environment, your staging,
your databases, your other features

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:03.360
that are connected to the specific feature
that you're working with.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:07.080
You understand that comparing code to code
isn't the answer.

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:08.640
You need to deploy the feature

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:11.640
in your staging environment
and really see how it looks like.

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:16.880
The good news is we don't need to in
like create any data that nobody has.

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:18.560
We have specs.

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:19.400
Specs are there.

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:21.560
And if you're a good team
and it's a really good practice,

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:23.600
you focus on writing specs.

00:07:25.520 --> 00:07:28.520
So this is my dream as a product manager.

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:31.320
This is the overview of the architecture

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:35.080
that I started to create together
with my CTO.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:38.520
I'm going to create an agent
that is called Spec Reviewer.

00:07:38.920 --> 00:07:41.880
It's going to have access to specs.

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:44.320
Specs will be saved in a ticketing system.

00:07:44.320 --> 00:07:48.080
It can be JIRA, it can be linear,
it can be Monday notion,

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:49.760
it can be on GitHub.

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:52.000
It will be able to access designs.

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:57.760
That is something that is not so standard
to visual assets in the designs,

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:01.800
and it will be able to access the feature
that is deployed in

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:05.600
staging environment
or in preview environment and verify it.

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:10.160
The way I see it is that agent is able
to extract the requirements, all of them,

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:13.400
and go every through every requirement

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:17.440
that are in the specs and validate
if they were really implemented or not.

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:19.240
I'm thinking about an agent

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:22.360
that is navigating
through different types, impersonating

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:26.240
to different roles into the product,
and just going and clicking

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:30.240
and trying different case
cases in the product.

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:35.160
So as a product manager, I'm already
coding, I have access to the best tools.

00:08:35.160 --> 00:08:37.280
And I'm going to ask Claude,

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:40.920
what does he think about this idea
and if he can implement it.

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:44.360
And the answer that I get is this.

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:48.160
And this is the moment
that I'm very concerned about, right?

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:51.680
Like when my wife tells me this,
I'm happy.

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:55.920
But when Claude tells me this,
I'm that's the point where I need to

00:08:56.080 --> 00:08:57.000
to be concerned.

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:59.720
And I click enter and I get this.

00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:01.760
The reason I'm

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:04.760
getting this is because it's
a very hard task to do.

00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:10.720
And you can see here this error shows
that the context window just exploded.

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:15.320
So what I'm going to show
now is how we took this simplistic idea

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:19.800
of implementing an agent
that is able to extract requirements

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:22.800
and verifying them
to make it actually work.

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:26.840
So this was the first problem.

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:30.080
The problem is that one
agent just can't do it.

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:34.280
One agent can take all the requirements
that are in the ticket,

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:36.200
all the requirements
that are in the specs,

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:38.600
all the design aspects
that were introduced in

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:39.960
this are the specific feature.

00:09:39.960 --> 00:09:42.080
And also go and verify them.

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:44.000
It just explodes the agent.

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:44.960
And by the way,

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:48.960
that's probably one of the reasons
that your coding agents don't do it.

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:50.840
So what was

00:09:50.840 --> 00:09:53.840
our solution to deal with the crashing
sessions?

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:59.480
The idea was to to divide the task
between two different agents.

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:05.560
It's also it's another best practice
you can use for other agent tasks.

00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:07.400
It's dividing it between planning.

00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:10.040
So we're going to have one agent
that is the planner.

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:13.600
The planners role
is to extract the requirements

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:17.840
from the spec and understand
what are going to be the failure cases

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:22.120
that I'm going to verify
through the verification process.

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:25.520
Only one task to extract requirements.

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:28.240
The second agent
is going to be the verification agent.

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:32.120
The verification agent is going
to navigate through different files,

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:36.960
through the UI, through the design,
and understand if the specific specs

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:41.960
that were provided by
the planner were were met in this feature.

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:47.000
Okay, good news sessions are running.

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:48.320
We're not crashing anymore,

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:52.000
but unfortunately, the results
that we're getting are horrible.

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:58.080
What we understood
is that the agents started

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:01.040
skipping some of the requirements.

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:05.000
We had tickets that included 10
or 12 requirements.

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:09.160
Sometimes we had the process
that included 20 requirements

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:14.000
and the agent
just consistently skipped some of them.

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:15.880
The second thing that happened

00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:19.640
was that we noticed
that the quality of the verification

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:24.400
and the quality of the requirements
that were extracted was inconsistent.

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:28.240
And we understood that
even though we divided the task between

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:31.360
two different agents,
we understood that it's

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:34.360
still too much context for one agent.

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:37.680
So this is going to be the only graph
that I'm going

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:40.880
to show in this presentation,
and I promise.

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:45.920
This is an illustration of the context
problem that we're having in this task.

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:50.880
So how the verification agent works,
it gets the list from the planner

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:54.480
of what is going to be the potential 
failure scenarios

00:11:54.480 --> 00:12:00.360
that I'm going to review, and starts
with the first requirement from the spec

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:05.560
going into specific files, navigating
through them, testing the UI, checking

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:11.200
if the requirement that is supposed to
check was implemented or not, defines

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:13.560
what would be the verdict,
and proceeds to the next one

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:14.960
and the next one, and the next one.

00:12:14.960 --> 00:12:18.400
You can understand that at this point,
when I'm getting to the fifth or the sixth

00:12:18.400 --> 00:12:21.920
requirement, I'm having loads of contexts
that are irrelevant

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:26.840
for that specific task,
and that makes the agent just dumber.

00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:29.440
The way to solve it

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:33.360
would be to delegate the task
between multiple subvariants.

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:37.200
It's understanding
that I can verify each of the sub

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:40.680
requirements in the spec
by different agents.

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:46.320
So instead of one agent that is checking
ten or 12 or 15 requirements

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:51.520
sequentially, I'm going to have 15
or 12 agents that are running in parallel.

00:12:51.560 --> 00:12:54.000
Each of them is reaching
to a specific verdict.

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:57.760
And in the end, there's an orchestrator
that collects all the verdicts

00:12:57.760 --> 00:13:00.760
at the end of the process.

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:02.040
Great.

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:03.760
We're having sessions that are running.

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:06.920
We have all the requirements
that are extracted and validated.

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:10.320
Now we started to notice another problem
that is related

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:13.360
to the quality of the context.

00:13:13.400 --> 00:13:18.000
We notice that the agent is starting
to make up

00:13:18.320 --> 00:13:21.320
requirements that nobody asked him to do.

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:27.200
This is like an example from one of
the first reports of our spec reviewer.

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:28.520
You can see it on GitHub here.

00:13:28.520 --> 00:13:32.480
For example, you can see here the agent
decided that we need to introduce

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:36.160
a new responder command
like nobody asked that.

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:40.080
The second example is maintaining
backward compatibility.

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:44.000
This is again
like something that the product manager,

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:45.880
the CTO that designed the specific

00:13:45.880 --> 00:13:49.760
task, like nobody really had
that specific requirement.

00:13:49.840 --> 00:13:54.640
The reason we understood what's happening
here is the fact that we gave the agent

00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:56.840
only the specs.

00:13:56.840 --> 00:14:01.720
And what's really interesting
here, and I added this git from inception,

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:05.480
is the fact that when we gave the
the agent the tickets,

00:14:05.480 --> 00:14:07.800
it only understood the specs
and the design.

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:11.960
It only understood
what's going to be the goal.

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:15.640
Like what is the intent
in that specific moment.

00:14:15.640 --> 00:14:20.640
But without to be grounded with the code,
it's just not connected to reality.

00:14:21.080 --> 00:14:25.520
The specs are a snapshot
and what we're trying to achieve

00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:29.240
in this coding task, but the code is

00:14:29.240 --> 00:14:32.240
how we ground that agent to reality.

00:14:32.560 --> 00:14:35.600
Two interesting takeaways in that area.

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:40.120
One, we understood that
if we give the agent the base branch

00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:44.560
and the base code and not the diff,
we're getting better results. Why?

00:14:44.600 --> 00:14:46.120
This was a really interesting case.

00:14:46.120 --> 00:14:49.880
We understood that if we're giving it
the diff, that it's biased

00:14:49.880 --> 00:14:53.520
to the specific solution
that the engineer choose to implement.

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:57.400
But if we give it the base branch
before the change, the agent is open

00:14:57.400 --> 00:14:59.120
minded to different kinds of approach

00:14:59.120 --> 00:15:03.320
and is more critical about the solution
that was was chosen.

00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:08.040
The second way to improve
that kind of results was to scope

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:11.680
the agent to understand
what it's reviewing right now.

00:15:11.720 --> 00:15:15.200
So, for example,
if I'm reviewing a front end feature,

00:15:15.240 --> 00:15:16.240
there's no reason to

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:20.480
be concerned about backend issues
because I'm just going to create noise

00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:22.680
that are irrelevant
for this specific feature.

00:15:24.640 --> 00:15:25.640
So the good news

00:15:25.640 --> 00:15:28.760
is we have sessions that are running,
we have requirements

00:15:28.760 --> 00:15:32.560
that are extracted perfectly
and we're verifying them amazingly.

00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:35.640
Now we're getting to a point
that is the really exciting point

00:15:35.640 --> 00:15:39.160
of sharing it with our customers that are
waiting for this specific feature.

00:15:39.560 --> 00:15:42.560
And we get to the point
where we're starting to plan

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:45.080
how the launch of this feature
is going to look like,

00:15:45.080 --> 00:15:47.080
and we get to the point
where we're saying, okay,

00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:50.080
now we need to integrate,
we need to let our customers

00:15:50.080 --> 00:15:54.000
integrate buzz
into their preview environment.

00:15:54.320 --> 00:16:00.200
So that means that buzz needs to access
multiple URLs, unknown ones.

00:16:00.200 --> 00:16:03.360
It can be teams that are just
giving us access to this URL.

00:16:03.440 --> 00:16:06.120
And you can understand that
that's that's not a good idea right?

00:16:06.120 --> 00:16:08.680
Like clicking on unknown URLs.

00:16:08.680 --> 00:16:12.440
It's just like clicking on phishing link
that I'm getting on an SMS on an email,

00:16:12.440 --> 00:16:16.280
and I'm going to do it 100 times a month.

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:20.600
My agent is sitting on our S3 bucket.

00:16:20.640 --> 00:16:23.840
Where our code is is where our data is.

00:16:23.840 --> 00:16:25.440
It's where our customers data is.

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:28.400
It's where our OpenAI Cardinals are.

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:33.720
That's not a good idea to run
arbitrary code on on my on my agent.

00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:37.360
So the way we decided to solve that
and again,

00:16:37.400 --> 00:16:39.520
this is our personal approach,
but I'm going to talk

00:16:39.520 --> 00:16:41.520
about how you can do it differently.

00:16:41.520 --> 00:16:44.520
It's using a third party tool.

00:16:44.600 --> 00:16:48.800
There's a lot of nonsense
people are doing there with coding agents.

00:16:48.800 --> 00:16:52.600
People are just vibe coding
a lot of a lot of software right now.

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:56.480
And if you're entering an area
that you think is a dangerous area

00:16:56.520 --> 00:17:01.360
related to cybersecurity abilities,
prefer using a third party tool

00:17:01.360 --> 00:17:05.200
instead of developing yourself,
there are this.

00:17:05.200 --> 00:17:10.160
This specific solution didn't really
impact the results that we generated.

00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:13.600
It just helped us deal with things
that we didn't want to deal with

00:17:13.640 --> 00:17:17.480
as as a dev tools company, in this case,
what we implemented

00:17:17.480 --> 00:17:21.840
and we used together with AWS
Agent Core, is the ability to run

00:17:21.840 --> 00:17:26.480
an ephemeral sandbox for every requirement
that is being validated.

00:17:26.520 --> 00:17:30.760
That means that when the agent
needs to validate a specific requirement,

00:17:30.760 --> 00:17:34.280
there will be a sandbox
that would be running

00:17:34.280 --> 00:17:37.280
for that specific requirement
with a browser session,

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:40.680
checking the specific feature
through the preview environment,

00:17:41.560 --> 00:17:44.560
sending back the verdict to the agent
that is in our cloud,

00:17:44.560 --> 00:17:46.160
and then the agent can understand

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:50.160
what's what was that specific requirement
if it was implemented or not.

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:54.280
And this is the dream become true.

00:17:54.280 --> 00:17:58.680
This is the moment where Spec reviewer
is running on our code.

00:17:58.680 --> 00:18:02.360
I waited for this moment
for close to six months,

00:18:02.440 --> 00:18:06.080
and you can see here multiple sessions
that are not human sessions,

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:09.080
but agent sessions
that are navigating through our UI.

00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:13.360
You can see here cases
where the agent is navigating

00:18:13.360 --> 00:18:17.000
through dashboards, checking
if data data integrity.

00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:20.200
When we added different features
to the dashboard,

00:18:20.240 --> 00:18:24.400
you can see cases where the agent
is looking for different integrations

00:18:24.400 --> 00:18:27.800
and trying to integrate
and activate a specific integration.

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:33.960
You can see the agent trying to subscribe
through stripe that sometimes breaks,

00:18:33.960 --> 00:18:37.120
and that's probably the most frustrating
point for you as a product manager.

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:40.560
When someone tries to pay
and the product isn't working, and also

00:18:40.560 --> 00:18:45.920
trying to subscribe to onboard
with a Google account or with so

00:18:45.920 --> 00:18:51.200
and this is running multiple times,
like dozens of times in every PR

00:18:51.200 --> 00:18:55.120
that we're doing to validate
every feature, to ensure regression tests,

00:18:55.120 --> 00:18:59.480
and to ensure that the features are
really implemented as we describe them.

00:19:02.840 --> 00:19:05.680
The last slide, the key takeaways.

00:19:05.680 --> 00:19:09.680
Or if you want to take this dream
and build it by yourself,

00:19:09.840 --> 00:19:14.000
first of all, it's 2026

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:17.120
and context
engineering is still a hard problem.

00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:21.000
There's a feeling that when you look at
what's going on

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:24.320
today, this is something it's so easy
to do, right?

00:19:24.360 --> 00:19:27.360
Like context windows are so big
and I have so many tokens.

00:19:27.360 --> 00:19:28.760
It's just so easy to do it.

00:19:28.760 --> 00:19:31.600
But the truth is, it's not complex.

00:19:31.600 --> 00:19:34.680
Tasks that are not out of the box
from coding agents

00:19:35.160 --> 00:19:38.840
require a lot of context engineering,
and two tips that I can give

00:19:38.840 --> 00:19:44.160
here was one dividing
between planning and execution,

00:19:44.160 --> 00:19:49.960
and the other one would be delegating
identity task between multiple subjects.

00:19:50.440 --> 00:19:51.200
The second.

00:19:51.200 --> 00:19:54.240
The second takeaway
here is the fact that you have your specs

00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:59.040
and you have your code, and you don't need
other resources except them.

00:19:59.040 --> 00:20:01.720
And if you combine them together,
it's really a gold mine.

00:20:01.720 --> 00:20:05.360
So you don't need to create more resources
to ensure that your agent

00:20:05.360 --> 00:20:06.600
is running smoothly.

00:20:06.600 --> 00:20:09.800
And the third takeaway is
the one that I talked about earlier.

00:20:09.800 --> 00:20:13.760
If you identify high area, high
risk areas

00:20:13.800 --> 00:20:17.880
that you think that can be vulnerable
in part of your SDLC

00:20:17.880 --> 00:20:21.440
or part of your architecture,
prefer using third party

00:20:21.640 --> 00:20:24.640
proven tools that you can use
instead of exposing yourself

00:20:24.640 --> 00:20:27.640
to security issues.

00:20:27.800 --> 00:20:30.080
The last step of way is a personal one.

00:20:30.080 --> 00:20:32.800
For someone that is a builder
and a product manager

00:20:32.800 --> 00:20:37.200
and is part of the startup life
these days can be very frustrating.

00:20:37.200 --> 00:20:41.280
I think for someone that is looking at
what's happening on Twitter

00:20:41.280 --> 00:20:47.160
or news can think that there's no way
to beat the big companies every day.

00:20:47.200 --> 00:20:50.840
Anthropic or cursor
or codex release a feature

00:20:50.840 --> 00:20:53.880
that you're saying like, oh damn, this is
this is what I'm thinking about.

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:57.720
This is going to be the big feature
that I was planning to do on next quarter.

00:20:57.720 --> 00:21:01.480
But the idea is that these

00:21:01.480 --> 00:21:06.480
big companies are overlooking
a lot of cracks

00:21:06.480 --> 00:21:10.800
that real teams are looking for products
to solve them.

00:21:10.880 --> 00:21:13.120
Spec reviewer is one of those examples

00:21:13.120 --> 00:21:17.320
that for me as a product manager,
it was mainly to tune in

00:21:17.400 --> 00:21:21.600
to the pains that I felt in our customers
and trying to find the place

00:21:21.600 --> 00:21:26.560
where coding agents don't really solve it,
and finding a way to build it.

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:28.280
And I think like this would be the best

00:21:28.280 --> 00:21:30.600
tip that I can give you
from this conversation.

00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:34.480
Look for the gaps that the big coding
agents are not able to to fill

00:21:34.480 --> 00:21:35.760
and build your product there.

00:21:35.760 --> 00:21:38.680
That will probably be the best way
for you to beat them.

00:21:38.680 --> 00:21:40.160
That's it.

